;;; A rule that could derive an activator binding a relation parameter to a
;;; fact, where no run derives one; the witness of a regression row that is
;;; gone, since the static check flags the program and no row holds a flagged
;;; program's answer (Q-M1f.564).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `derived-activator.ein` without `(item (a b))`: `mk` derives `r`'s
;;; activator `(r ?f)` from `item`'s argument, a declared relation's, which can
;;; hold a fact, and `?f` is `r`'s parameter `?R`, which names a relation. No
;;; fact and no candidate puts a fact in `item`, so no run derives one.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The program loaded and answered, exit 0, in silence (`ein solve FILE` at
;;; `8249e872`, release and debug alike). The loader then refused it, as it
;;; refused `derived-activator.ein`, exit 1.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Every command loads the program, exit 0, and says nothing of `mk`,
;;; which could derive an activator binding `?R` to a fact, one that would
;;; activate nothing (Q-M1f.1); no state derives one. The static check
;;; flags the program, so nothing is claimed of its answer. Its twin, where
;;; root derives one, says nothing of it either.
;;;

(relation box Node :why "{?3} is in the box")
(relation hit Node :why "{?3} is hit")
(relation item Node :why "{?3} is an item")
(box (a b))
(rule r (?R)
  :match (box (?R b))
  :assert (hit z)
  :why "a box holds a {?R} of b")
(rule mk ()
  :match (item ?f)
  :assert (r ?f)
  :why "{?f} is an item, so r is asked of it")
(query :goal (hit ?x))
